what exactly breaks, and why the label is mandatory.

🔹 Full program (reference)
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math/rand"
)

func main() {
randloop:
	for {
		switch i := rand.Intn(100); {
		case i%2 == 0:
			fmt.Printf("Generated even number %d", i)
			break randloop
		}
	}
}

1️⃣ What problem are we solving?

Keep generating random numbers until we get an even one, then stop everything.

So we need:

repeated execution → loop

condition checking → switch

early termination → break outer loop

2️⃣ Why an infinite for loop
for {

Syntax meaning

No init

No condition

No post

📌 This is Go’s infinite loop (like while(true)).

Why infinite?

We don’t know how many attempts it will take

We stop only when a condition is met

3️⃣ Why the loop is labeled
randloop:
	for {

What this does

randloop is a label

It names the for loop

Allows us to target this loop explicitly

📌 Labels apply to the statement immediately after them.

4️⃣ The switch with assignment
switch i := rand.Intn(100); {

Syntax breakdown

i := rand.Intn(100)

Generates random number in range 0–99

i exists only inside the switch

No expression → switch true

Equivalent to:

i := rand.Intn(100)
switch true {
	...
}

5️⃣ Why switch is used instead of if

You could write:

if i%2 == 0 {
	...
}


But switch is used here to demonstrate control-flow behavior, especially break with labels.

📌 In real code, both are valid.

6️⃣ Case condition
case i%2 == 0:


% → modulus operator

i%2 == 0 → checks even number

If true → case executes.

7️⃣ The critical line: break randloop
break randloop

What exactly happens here

📌 This breaks the labeled for loop, NOT the switch.

Execution steps:

break randloop is encountered

Go looks for label randloop

Finds it on the for

Exits the for loop immediately

Program ends

8️⃣ Why plain break is NOT enough

If you wrote:

break


Then:

It would break only the switch

Control returns to for

Loop continues forever ❌

📌 Rule

break without label breaks the nearest enclosing structure.

Nearest here = switch.

9️⃣ Control-flow hierarchy (IMPORTANT)

Inside this code:

for {
	switch {
		case ...
	}
}


Order of nesting:

switch

for

So:

break → exits switch

break randloop → exits for

🔟 Step-by-step execution

Let’s say random numbers generated are:

13 → odd → nothing happens → loop continues
27 → odd → continue
18 → even → print → break randloop → EXIT


Output:

Generated even number 18

11️⃣ Why Go needs labels here (design reason)

Go does not support:

break 2

break outer

Instead, Go uses:
✔ labels
✔ explicit control
✔ no ambiguity

This avoids bugs common in other languages.

12️⃣ Real-world backend patterns (this is NOT artificial)
🔹 Retry until success
retry:
	for {
		switch try() {
		case success:
			break retry
		}
	}

🔹 Polling job status
poll:
	for {
		switch status() {
		case "DONE":
			break poll
		}
	}

🔹 Worker loop exit
worker:
	for {
		switch msg.Type {
		case "STOP":
			break worker
		}
	}

13️⃣ Rules to remember (save this)

for {} → infinite loop

Labels name loops or blocks

break label exits that labeled structure

Plain break exits nearest switch/loop

Required when switch is inside for

Very common in real Go systems

🧠 Mental model (lock this in)

Label = target
break label = jump out of that target

✅ Final takeaway

This is idiomatic Go

Used in real systems

Necessary for clean exit from nested control flow

Safer than flags or hacks